3:1-13 (* Parse is a recursive descent over report par 10, in M9, restated\n   from host/fpc/M9Parse.pas for P5 stage 1.  ERRORS ARE VALUES: every\n   one lands in the parser as line, col and a message, in the oracle's\n   own wording, and a tree always comes back.\n\n   The messages were the stage-2 work this header used to promise and\n   defer: "the count is all there is today".  The deferral cost a\n   bisect every time a program was written outside the corpus, most\n   recently inside the tutorial.  There a reader following the\n   chapter's own advice got `1 parse errors` and no line.  It was\n   written 2026-08-30.                                               *)
20:3-24 (* The parser KEEPS the first MaxErr and COUNTS all of them.  It uses\n     a fixed array rather than a pool: Init takes no pool (the Parser is\n     a record the caller owns, and Doc/M9c/parsetest all rely on that),\n     and a module-level list would make this module STATEFUL for the\n     sake of a diagnostic.  Nothing reads past the eighth. *)
26:3-33 (* MaxDepth is the deepest tree the parser builds: a level for each\n     nested expression, statement or type and for each further operand\n     of a chain like 1 + 1 + ... (2026-10-21, the compiler review's F3).\n     Every pass after the parser walks the tree by recursion, and a\n     source nested 12,000 deep or a sum of 60,000 terms ran the stack\n     out with no message.  Under a 1 MB stack the walks reach 1,000 and\n     not 2,000, so a deeper source is refused here, where the line is\n     known. *)
35:3-39 (* MaxTypeDepth is the deepest TYPE.  A type's text is carried whole\n     at every level it is resolved through, so `SLICE OF' 990 deep took\n     0.66 GB and `ARRAY 2 OF' 1.1 GB within MaxDepth.  100 levels is a\n     few megabytes, and no type written to be read nests a tenth of\n     it. *)
46:3-52 (* Perr is one kept error.  Parser holds an ARRAY OF it, bounded by\n     MaxErr.  The parser kept three parallel arrays with the literal 64\n     until 2026-10-10, because the generator wrote the array's typedef\n     after every struct (gcc: unknown type name) and read the bare bound\n     in an IMPORTER's namespace (array count form unsupported).  Both\n     are fixed in both generators; genforms' GfArrRecField holds\n     them. *)
57:32-57 (* every error *)
58:32-58 (* those kept: MIN (nerr, MaxErr) *)
60:32-60 (* levels open, against MaxDepth *)
61:32-61 (* type levels open, against MaxTypeDepth *)
62:32-62 (* a bound passed: the rest is EOF *)
66:3-72 (* Init points a parser at source text and reads the first token.\n\n       src -- BORROWED for as long as p is used, and for as long as\n              the tree p builds is used: identifier and literal text\n              in the AST are SLICES of this, not copies.  The\n              caller's buffer must outlive both, which is the same\n              contract Json.Parse has with its document.          *)
74:3-75 (* InitUtf is Init over a UTF, the same contract; the text is lexed\n     as it is. *)
78:3-90 (* File parses a whole file and answers its NFile node, whose kids are\n     the definition and implementation units in source order.\n\n     ERRORS ARE VALUES and the tree is always answered: the parser\n     resyncs on ';' and END rather than stopping, so one mistake\n     does not hide the rest of the file.  Check `p.nerr` before\n     trusting the tree.  The record is transparent for exactly\n     that, and a caller that skips the check gets a tree with holes\n     in it and no warning.  p.errs[0 .. p.nkept - 1]\n     carry the first MaxErr of them; p.nerr counts all.  A caller\n     prints them as `FILE:LINE:COL: parse: MSG`.\n\n       pool -- owns every node.  The tree cannot outlive it.      *)
102:3-102 (* These are the token kind codes, matching Lex. *)
121:3-121 (* appended: codes are identifiers *)
122:3-123 (* operators start at 200: see the note in Lex.  Keywords may be\n     appended at 65.. without moving any of these.                *)
131:3-132 (* These are stop-set ids.  M9 has no set type, so each distinct\n     stop set is a named predicate case. *)
133:25-133 (* END *)
134:25-134 (* EXCEPT FINALLY END *)
135:25-135 (* ELSIF ELSE END *)
136:25-136 (* | FINALLY END *)
137:25-137 (* | ELSE END *)
138:25-138 (* ) *)
139:25-139 (* | END *)
204:1-208 (* Spell answers THE SPELLING of a token kind, which is what a message\n   says was expected: 'MODULE', ';', ':='.  Lex.KindName answers the\n   kind's NAME ('Semi'), which is what a message says was FOUND.  The\n   two are different questions and the oracle asks both.  Keywords spell\n   themselves, so KwName is the whole answer for them. *)
225:5-227 (* a KEYWORD spells itself, and Lex.KindName already answers the\n       keyword table for those, so the fallback is the whole rest of\n       the answer *)
232:1-242 (* A RETAINED MESSAGE MUST BE COPIED, and this is the one place that\n   decides it.  Every caller composes its text with `+`, and the\n   answer lives wherever the caller's concatenation put it.  That\n   was HEAP, and is the caller's FRAME once frame pools land\n   (docs/frame-pools.md).  A Parser outlives the procedure that\n   reported into it, so keeping the caller's slice is a borrow held\n   past its frame: par 4.1 retention, and ownledger has named this\n   exact line for months (`Parse.ErrAt: retention: borrowed msg\n   stored into p`).  HEAP is the right home: a compiler's\n   diagnostics die with the process, which is what HEAP is for.\n   And at most MaxErr of them are ever kept. *)
245:3-246 (* past MaxDepth the parser unwinds through an EOF it made: what it\n     says on the way out is about that EOF, not the source *)
257:1-258 (* Rerr reports at the current token, which is where all but the\n   lexical ones are. *)
296:1-298 (* the FPC oracle's Expect returned a Boolean that every call site\n   discarded; the M9 checker refuses discarded results, so the port\n   is honest: a proper procedure *)
304:5-306 (* the oracle passes the spelling at all 83 call sites; here it\n       is DERIVED from the kind, which is the same string and cannot\n       drift from the token it names *)
312:1-316 (* `what` names the identifier the grammar wanted -- 'module name',\n   'field name' -- because "identifier expected" in a file with\n   twenty identifiers on the line says nothing.  They are the\n   oracle's own phrases, kept word for word so the two parsers can be\n   held to the same diagnostics. *)
317:1-318 (* TakeIdent answers the identifier's text, a view of the source\n   (Ast.SetA copies it into the node). *)
332:1-335 (* Deeper opens one more level.  Past MaxDepth it makes the one\n   refusal, and the parser sees the end of the file from here on, so\n   it returns through every level it opened without opening\n   another. *)
352:1-353 (* Started sets the state once the lexer is pointed: no error, no\n   depth, the first token read. *)
376:1-376 (* These are forward heads for the mutual recursion. *)
447:1-457 (* PNew parses NEW (T), NEW (T, n...), NEW (pool, T, n...) and\n   NEW (OWN, T).  The first argument says who frees the storage:\n   nothing (the frame, par 4.3), a pool's name, or OWN (one binding,\n   DISPOSE).  A pool and a\n   type are both spelled as a designator, so `NEW (a, b)` is resolved\n   by NAME in the checkers and generators, not here (report par 10,\n   item 6).  kids[0] is the first argument or NONE, kids[1] the second\n   as an EXPRESSION (a type name in the pool and OWN forms, an extent\n   in the frame form), kids[2..] the rest, with a NONE in kids[2] when\n   there is no third.  The single-argument form keeps kids[1] as a\n   qualident, as it always was. *)
482:9-483 (* further extents make it a GRID: the arity states the rank,\n           so the rank cannot disagree with what the caller gave *)
501:1-502 (* Closes asks whether the token ends a statement, a list or a\n   construct. *)
544:5-544 (* GRID (s, n0, ..., nR): a grid laid over a slice (par 2.2.1) *)
586:7-586 (* F (x).f, F (x)[i]: the call's answer selected from *)
600:5-604 (* A token that ends a statement or a construct is left for\n       whoever owns it.  A parser that consumed the `;' of `x := 1 + ;'\n       made the next statement an error too, and a literal the lexer\n       refused cascaded to 65 errors (2026-10-22, the compiler\n       review's tier 3). *)
635:3-637 (* Keep the sign and its operand on one path.  Under a flag set by\n     one IF and read by another, definite assignment cannot see that\n     sgn was given a value. *)
729:7-731 (* `x IS SOME p' binds, as the whole condition of IF/ELSIF/WHILE;\n         `x IS SOME' and `x IS NONE' are BOOL expressions, no binder\n         (cp-kernel's issue 4, 2026-10-09) *)
757:1-758 (* PSels appends to n the selectors that follow a name, or a call's\n   closing parenthesis (NCallSel, 2026-10-09). *)
769:7-770 (* one subscript per axis: a[i] on a slice, a[i, j, k] on a\n         GRID, and the arity is checked against the rank *)
791:5-791 (* [DEPRECATED 'text'] carries a string: what to write instead *)
868:5-869 (* GRID r OF T: the rank is in the type so that subscript arity\n       is a compile-time question, the extents are in the value *)
881:33-881 (* RO is a mode now; kid kept for shape *)
895:5-896 (* RECORD ... END [SHARABLE]: threads may share it, their writes\n       disjoint by the author's claim (report par 6, 2026-10-11) *)
939:5-942 (* This parses an enumeration: (Red, Green, Blue), a member per\n       kid, in declaration order, ordinals 0..n-1 (docs/enum-plan.md).\n       It takes no explicit values: those are protocol numbers, and a\n       wire format keeps its CONSTs. *)
957:5-960 (* This parses a procedure type: PROCEDURE (params) [: T]\n       [RAISES ...], the head of a ProcDecl without a name.  It has the\n       same three kids, so the checker's parameter walk serves both\n       (docs/proctype-plan.md). *)
1007:1-1017 (* QualPattern reads a QUALIFIED variant with binders,\n   `Frame.Data.F64s (v, m)', as the call it looks like and turns it\n   into the pattern it is: the callee nothing but field selectors,\n   every argument a bare name.\n   The one-token lookahead cannot see past `Frame.' to the `(', so the\n   label went down as an expression and its binders as unknown names\n   (OneFlux, 2026-10-19; the m9 skill's own example is the qualified\n   form).  The variant is the last selector; the type the\n   qualification names is the selector's, which the checker holds the\n   label to as for the bare form.  It answers NONE when E is anything\n   else. *)
1048:7-1050 (* bu keeps what qualified the variant, `Frame.Data', so that the\n         printer writes the label as it was written.  Nothing else\n         reads it: a variant is matched by its own name. *)
1188:5-1191 (* FOR [INRANGE b] v := lo TO hi: the author claims the loop's\n       subscripts are in range (2026-11-15, docs/loop-check-plan.md).\n       The claim stands after FOR, since after `hi' a bracket is a\n       subscript.  It is the node's fifth child. *)
1202:5-1203 (* FOR x IN s DO ... END, over a slice's or an array's elements\n       (2026-10-11) *)
1289:5-1290 (* A TASK is a THREAD node with au 'TASK'.  The checker holds both\n       to the same rules by the same code (docs/task-plan.md). *)
1365:3-1366 (* one arm for several exceptions, `| A, B :' (2026-10-11): the\n     further names go after the body, kids[3..] *)
1450:5-1452 (* KEPT composes rather than replacing: a parameter this procedure\n       keeps past the call is usually also a read-only borrow, so it\n       follows the mode instead of being one (docs/retention.md) *)
1518:5-1518 (* RO precedes what it qualifies, in return position too *)
1543:1-1548 (* PAggregate parses Aggregate = "[" ConstExpr { "," ConstExpr } "]":\n   the value of a CONST and nothing else (report par 10).  The\n   elements are parsed as expressions.  The checker says, with a\n   position, whether they are constants and of one type.  An empty\n   list is a parse error: an aggregate with no element has no element\n   type. *)
1553:52-1553 (* consume '[' *)
1575:7-1576 (* An attribute goes before the =: after the value, `0 [' would\n         read as an index. *)
1702:5-1705 (* This is a placeholder until LINK makes the list.  hasLinks\n       guards every use, and a PTR that is not OPT is given a value\n       before it can be read (par 4, definite assignment, 2026-10-21).\n       It is a node of its own, since `links := n' would move n. *)
1708:7-1710 (* LINK "w", ... says what the foreign unit links (par 7).  The\n         node goes in LAST, after the declarations, so that nothing\n         walking a unit's kids by position moves. *)
1748:7-1749 (* a BLOCK: the root frame must be able to say what it does\n         about failure.  PBlock consumes BEGIN .. END. *)
1771:7-1772 (* a BLOCK: the root frame must be able to say what it does\n         about failure.  PBlock consumes BEGIN .. END. *)
1801:5-1805 (* a file has at most one PROGRAM module.  A second bare MODULE\n       is another main, and without this it silently WON: `MODULE\n       Hello. MODULE Welcome.` compiled and ran Welcome, discarding\n       Hello.  A program may still be FOLLOWED by a DEFINITION or a\n       FOR-C unit it uses (serialtest is a program then cserial). *)
comments=68
